    <h2 id="intro">Introduction — Why Network Defense Matters</h2>

    <p>Imagine your organization's computer network as a city. Streets carry traffic, buildings store valuable assets,
      and citizens (users) move around every day. Without walls, guards, gates, or ID checks, anyone could walk in,
      steal data, sabotage systems, or spy on communications. In the digital world, <strong>firewalls, IDS/IPS systems,
        VPNs, and access controls</strong> are the equivalent of city walls, surveillance cameras, encrypted courier
      tunnels, and ID verification desks.</p>

    <p>As of 2024, cybercrime costs the global economy over <strong>$8 trillion per year</strong>, with a major breach
      occurring every 39 seconds on average. Every organization — from a small startup to a government agency — must
      understand and deploy these four critical defense technologies. This guide walks you through each one: what it is,
      how it works, why it matters, and where it fails.</p>

    <div class="callout analogy">
      <div class="callout-title">🏰 The Castle Analogy</div>
      Think of your network as a medieval castle. The <strong>firewall</strong> is the outer wall with guarded gates.
      The <strong>IDS/IPS</strong> is the patrol of guards watching for suspicious behavior inside and outside. The
      <strong>VPN</strong> is a secret underground tunnel that ambassadors use to communicate securely. And
      <strong>access control</strong> is the system of keys, passes, and permissions that decides who can enter each
      room.
    </div>

