    <h2 id="firewalls">Firewalls — The Gatekeepers</h2>

    <h3>What is a Firewall?</h3>
    <p>A <strong>firewall</strong> is a network security system — either hardware, software, or both — that monitors and
      controls incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predetermined security rules. It establishes a barrier
      between a trusted internal network and untrusted external networks, such as the Internet.</p>

    <p>The term "firewall" is borrowed from construction: a physical firewall is a wall designed to stop the spread of
      fire from one room to another. In computing, it stops the "fire" (malicious traffic) from spreading into your
      network.</p>

    <h3>A Brief History of Firewalls</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1988:</strong> The first paper on firewall technology was written after the Morris Worm — one of the
        first recognized internet worms — infected thousands of computers.</li>
      <li><strong>1989–1990:</strong> The first packet-filtering routers appeared, developed by engineers at Digital
        Equipment Corporation (DEC).</li>
      <li><strong>1991:</strong> AT&amp;T Bell Labs developed the first stateful inspection firewall — a major leap forward.
      </li>
      <li><strong>1994:</strong> Check Point Software released FireWall-1, the first commercially available firewall.
      </li>
      <li><strong>2000s:</strong> Next-Generation Firewalls (NGFWs) emerged, incorporating deep packet inspection,
        application awareness, and integrated intrusion prevention.</li>
      <li><strong>2010s–present:</strong> Cloud-native firewalls, AI-driven rule engines, and Software-Defined
        Networking (SDN) firewalls become mainstream.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>How a Firewall Works — Step by Step</h3>

    <div class="step-flow">
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num">1</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Packet Arrives</strong>
          <p>A data packet arrives at the firewall from either the internet (inbound) or from inside the network
            (outbound). Every packet contains a source IP, destination IP, port number, and protocol.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num">2</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Rule Matching</strong>
          <p>The firewall checks the packet against its ruleset — an ordered list of ALLOW and DENY rules. Rules are
            matched top to bottom; the first match wins.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num">3</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>State Check (Stateful Firewalls)</strong>
          <p>Stateful firewalls also check whether this packet belongs to an existing, established connection. If yes,
            it's far more likely to be legitimate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num">4</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Action Taken</strong>
          <p>The firewall either <strong>allows</strong> the packet through, <strong>drops</strong> it silently, or
            <strong>rejects</strong> it with an error message back to the sender.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="step-item">
        <div class="step-num">5</div>
        <div class="step-content">
          <strong>Logging</strong>
          <p>The decision is logged for auditing, compliance, and threat analysis.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

