    <h2 id="fw-types">Types of Firewalls Explained</h2>

    <h3>1. Packet-Filtering Firewall</h3>
    <p>The oldest and simplest type. It inspects packets at the network layer (Layer 3) based solely on header
      information: source/destination IP, port numbers, and protocol (TCP/UDP/ICMP). Each packet is evaluated
      independently — there is no memory of past packets.</p>
    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">Example Rule</div>
      DENY TCP from ANY to 192.168.1.0/24 port 23 — This blocks all Telnet connections (port 23) to the internal subnet,
      because Telnet sends data in plain text.
    </div>
    <div class="pros-cons">
      <div class="pros">
        <h4>✅ Advantages</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Extremely fast — low latency</li>
          <li>Simple to configure</li>
          <li>Very low resource usage</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cons">
        <h4>❌ Disadvantages</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>No session awareness</li>
          <li>Easily bypassed with IP spoofing</li>
          <li>Cannot detect payload-level attacks</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>2. Stateful Inspection Firewall</h3>
    <p>Introduced in the 1990s, stateful firewalls maintain a <strong>connection state table</strong>. They remember
      which connections have been established and can validate that incoming packets are part of a legitimate, ongoing
      conversation. A response packet is allowed only if an outbound request was made first.</p>
    <div class="callout success">
      <div class="callout-title">Real-World Analogy</div>
      A stateful firewall is like a receptionist who remembers who called in and only puts through callbacks from those
      exact numbers. A packet-filtering firewall just reads the name tag — it doesn't know if you were expected.
    </div>

    <h3>3. Application Layer Firewall (Proxy Firewall)</h3>
    <p>Also called a <strong>proxy firewall</strong>, this type operates at Layer 7 (Application Layer) of the OSI
      model. It acts as an intermediary — all traffic must pass through the proxy, which terminates the connection,
      inspects the full payload, and re-establishes a new connection to the destination. This allows deep inspection of
      HTTP, FTP, SMTP, and DNS traffic.</p>

    <h3>4. Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW)</h3>
    <p>NGFWs combine traditional firewall capabilities with additional features including deep packet inspection (DPI),
      integrated intrusion detection/prevention, SSL/TLS decryption, application identification (even when running on
      non-standard ports), and user identity awareness. They are the current industry standard for enterprise networks.
    </p>

    <h3>5. Web Application Firewall (WAF)</h3>
    <p>A specialized firewall designed specifically for web applications. WAFs protect against application-layer attacks
      such as SQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), and CSRF attacks. They inspect HTTP/HTTPS traffic at the
      content level and can apply custom rules for specific applications.</p>

