    <h2 id="ids-ips">IDS and IPS — The Watchdogs</h2>

    <h3>What are IDS and IPS?</h3>
    <p>While a firewall is a gatekeeper deciding who gets in, an <strong>Intrusion Detection System (IDS)</strong> is a
      security camera network — it watches everything happening inside and raises an alarm when it spots something
      suspicious. An <strong>Intrusion Prevention System (IPS)</strong> goes one step further: not only does it detect
      the intrusion, it actively blocks it in real time.</p>

    <div class="callout analogy">
      <div class="callout-title">🚨 The Guard Analogy</div>
      IDS = A security guard who watches CCTV and calls the police when they see a break-in. IPS = A security guard who
      physically tackles the intruder before they can do anything. The IDS tells you what happened; the IPS stops it
      from happening.
    </div>

    <h3>Key Differences: IDS vs IPS</h3>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Feature</th>
            <th>IDS</th>
            <th>IPS</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Position in network</td>
            <td>Out-of-band (passive)</td>
            <td>Inline (active)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Response to threat</td>
            <td>Alerts / logs only</td>
            <td>Blocks + alerts + logs</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Effect on traffic</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>Can drop/modify packets</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>False positive risk</td>
            <td>Low disruption</td>
            <td>Can block legit traffic</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Latency added</td>
            <td>None</td>
            <td>Minimal but present</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Best use case</td>
            <td>Monitoring, forensics</td>
            <td>Active threat prevention</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

