    <h2 id="ids-types">IDS/IPS Detection Methods &amp; Types</h2>

    <h3>Detection Methods</h3>

    <h4>Signature-Based Detection</h4>
    <p>This is the most common method. The system maintains a database of known attack patterns ("signatures") — similar
      to how antivirus software works. Incoming traffic is compared against these signatures. If a match is found, an
      alert is triggered.</p>
    <div class="pros-cons">
      <div class="pros">
        <h4>✅ Pros</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Very accurate for known threats</li>
          <li>Low false-positive rate</li>
          <li>Fast to match signatures</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="cons">
        <h4>❌ Cons</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Cannot detect <em>new</em> (zero-day) attacks</li>
          <li>Signatures must be constantly updated</li>
          <li>Attackers can slightly mutate attacks to evade</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4>Anomaly-Based Detection</h4>
    <p>The system first learns what "normal" traffic looks like for your network (a baseline). Any deviation from this
      baseline — unusual port usage, spike in traffic volume, strange connection patterns — triggers an alert. It can
      detect zero-day attacks but produces more false positives.</p>

    <h4>Stateful Protocol Analysis</h4>
    <p>The IDS compares network behavior to pre-defined profiles of how protocols (HTTP, FTP, DNS) should behave. If a
      protocol is being used in an unexpected way — e.g., DNS tunneling — it raises an alert.</p>

    <h3>Deployment Types</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Network-based IDS/IPS (NIDS/NIPS):</strong> Deployed at strategic points in the network to monitor all
        passing traffic. Best for broad visibility.</li>
      <li><strong>Host-based IDS/IPS (HIDS/HIPS):</strong> Installed on individual devices. Monitors system calls, file
        integrity, log files, and running processes on that specific host. Best for detecting insider threats and
        malware.</li>
      <li><strong>Wireless IDS (WIDS):</strong> Monitors wireless network traffic and detects rogue access points,
        deauthentication attacks, and other Wi-Fi-based threats.</li>
      <li><strong>Network Behavior Analysis (NBA):</strong> Monitors network flows (not individual packets) to detect
        DDoS attacks, port scanning, and unusual bandwidth usage.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout warning">
      <div class="callout-title">⚠ The False Positive Problem</div>
      A major challenge with IDS/IPS systems is "alert fatigue" — when systems generate too many false positives,
      security analysts become desensitized and may start ignoring alerts. A well-tuned IPS is critical: too sensitive
      and it blocks legitimate traffic; too lenient and threats slip through.
    </div>

