    <h2 id="vpn-types">VPN Types and Protocols</h2>

    <h3>VPN Deployment Types</h3>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Remote Access VPN:</strong> Allows individual users to connect securely to a corporate network from
        anywhere. Used by remote employees accessing internal systems. The user's device acts as a VPN client connecting
        to a centralized VPN gateway.</li>
      <li><strong>Site-to-Site VPN:</strong> Connects entire networks to each other — for example, a headquarters office
        to a branch office. Both sides have VPN gateways; individual users don't need VPN software. Traffic between
        sites flows automatically through the tunnel.</li>
      <li><strong>Client-to-Client VPN:</strong> Creates direct encrypted connections between user devices (used in
        peer-to-peer applications and some gaming networks).</li>
      <li><strong>Consumer VPN (Privacy VPN):</strong> Used by individuals to hide their IP address from websites and
        ISPs, bypass geographic content restrictions, and encrypt traffic on public Wi-Fi.</li>
    </ul>

