    <h2 id="access">Access Control — Who Gets In?</h2>

    <h3>What is Access Control?</h3>
    <p>Access control is the practice of <strong>restricting access to resources</strong> — systems, data, applications,
      or physical spaces — to only those who are authorized. In network security, it answers three fundamental
      questions:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Authentication:</strong> Who are you? (Prove your identity)</li>
      <li><strong>Authorization:</strong> What are you allowed to do? (Your permissions)</li>
      <li><strong>Accounting/Auditing:</strong> What did you actually do? (Activity log)</li>
    </ol>

    <p>Together, these three principles form the <strong>AAA framework</strong> — the cornerstone of access control in
      any secure system.</p>

    <div class="callout analogy">
      <div class="callout-title">🏢 The Office Building Analogy</div>
      <strong>Authentication</strong> = showing your employee badge at the door. <strong>Authorization</strong> = your
      badge only opens certain floors (you can't get to the R&amp;D lab unless you have clearance).
      <strong>Accounting</strong> = the building logs every door you swipe through and at what time.
    </div>

    <h3>Authentication Factors</h3>
    <p>Modern authentication uses one or more of these factor types:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Something you know:</strong> Password, PIN, security question</li>
      <li><strong>Something you have:</strong> Hardware token, smart card, mobile authenticator app (TOTP)</li>
      <li><strong>Something you are:</strong> Biometrics — fingerprint, retina scan, facial recognition, voice
        recognition</li>
      <li><strong>Somewhere you are:</strong> Location-based (IP geolocation, GPS) — login only from known office IP
      </li>
      <li><strong>Something you do:</strong> Behavioral biometrics — typing cadence, mouse movement patterns</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA)</strong> combines two or more of these factors. Even if an attacker
      steals your password (factor 1), they still cannot log in without your phone (factor 2).</p>

    <!-- SECTION 9: ACCESS MODELS -->
