    <h2 id="access-models">Access Control Models</h2>

    <h3>1. Discretionary Access Control (DAC)</h3>
    <p>The resource owner decides who can access their resources. For example, a file owner on a Unix/Linux system can
      set read/write/execute permissions for themselves, their group, and everyone else. DAC is flexible but risky —
      users can grant excessive permissions to others.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">Example — DAC in Action</div>
      On a shared Linux server, Alice creates a financial spreadsheet and grants Bob read access. She is in full control
      of who can access her file. If she accidentally makes it world-readable, anyone on the system can see it.
    </div>

    <h3>2. Mandatory Access Control (MAC)</h3>
    <p>A central authority (the system or administrator) assigns <strong>security labels</strong> to every subject
      (user/process) and object (file/resource). Access is granted only when the subject's clearance level matches or
      exceeds the object's classification. Common in military and government systems: Top Secret, Secret, Confidential,
      Unclassified.</p>

    <h3>3. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC)</h3>
    <p>Users are assigned <strong>roles</strong> (e.g., Admin, Editor, Viewer, Manager), and permissions are granted to
      roles rather than individuals. When a user's job function changes, their role changes — the underlying permissions
      don't need to be individually updated. RBAC is the most widely used model in enterprise applications.</p>

