    <h3>4. Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC)</h3>
    <p>The most flexible and granular model. Access decisions are based on attributes of the user (department, clearance
      level, time of day, location), the resource (classification level, owner, type), and the environment (network
      type, time, device). ABAC policies can express very fine-grained rules: "Allow access to patient records only for
      nurses, only during working hours, only from hospital-registered devices."</p>

    <h3>5. Zero Trust Architecture</h3>
    <p>Traditional security assumes everything inside the network perimeter is trusted. <strong>Zero Trust</strong>
      rejects this assumption entirely. Its core principle is <em>"Never trust, always verify."</em> Every access
      request — regardless of where it comes from (even internal) — must be authenticated, authorized, and continuously
      validated.</p>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-title">🔐 Zero Trust Pillars</div>
      <strong>Verify explicitly:</strong> Always authenticate and authorize based on all available data points.
      <br><strong>Use least privilege access:</strong> Limit user access with just-in-time and just-enough-access.
      <br><strong>Assume breach:</strong> Minimize blast radius, segment access, verify end-to-end encryption.
    </div>

