    <h2 id="case-studies">Real-World Case Studies</h2>

    <div class="case-study">
      <h4>Case Study 01</h4>
      <h3>The 2020 SolarWinds Attack — When Firewalls Weren't Enough</h3>
      <p>In December 2020, it was revealed that Russian intelligence hackers (Cozy Bear/APT29) had compromised the
        SolarWinds Orion software update mechanism. Malicious code was inserted into legitimate software updates, which
        18,000 organizations downloaded and installed — bypassing perimeter firewalls entirely, because the traffic came
        from a trusted, signed software update.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>What went wrong:</strong> Firewalls only filter network traffic, not software supply chains. Once
          the malicious code was inside, it behaved like legitimate monitoring software.</li>
        <li><strong>What would have helped:</strong> Zero Trust architecture — the malware's unusual lateral movement
          patterns would have triggered UEBA (User and Entity Behavior Analytics) alerts.</li>
        <li><strong>Lesson:</strong> Defense in depth and Zero Trust are not optional — no single perimeter control is
          sufficient against sophisticated, patient attackers.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <h4>Case Study 02</h4>
      <h3>Operation Aurora (2009) — Google's Response and IPS Evolution</h3>
      <p>In 2009, Google and 30+ other major corporations were targeted by a sophisticated cyberattack originating from
        China. Attackers exploited a zero-day vulnerability in Internet Explorer to gain access to Gmail accounts of
        Chinese human rights activists and intellectual property from tech companies.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Firewalls failed:</strong> The attack came through legitimate HTTPS traffic — signature-based IDS
          systems had no signature for the zero-day.</li>
        <li><strong>IPS helped catch the exfiltration:</strong> Anomaly-based IPS noticed unusual volumes of data
          leaving the network at odd hours — a behavior deviation from the normal baseline.</li>
        <li><strong>Outcome:</strong> Google moved to a Zero Trust model they called "BeyondCorp" — eliminating the
          trusted internal network concept entirely.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="case-study">
      <h4>Case Study 03</h4>
      <h3>Colonial Pipeline (2021) — VPN Credentials and Access Control Failure</h3>
      <p>In May 2021, the Colonial Pipeline company — operator of the largest fuel pipeline in the US — was hit by a
        DarkSide ransomware attack that led to fuel shortages across the East Coast. The attacker gained entry using a
        single compromised VPN account password found on the dark web.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Root cause:</strong> The compromised VPN account did not have multi-factor authentication enabled. A
          stolen password was all that was needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Access control failure:</strong> Once inside via VPN, the account had broader access than necessary
          (violated least privilege principle).</li>
        <li><strong>Cost:</strong> Colonial paid a $4.4 million ransom. The FBI later recovered $2.3 million.</li>
        <li><strong>Lesson:</strong> MFA on all VPN accounts is non-negotiable. Least-privilege access limits the blast
          radius of a compromised account.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

